WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:49.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. There's a storefront at a major intersection in downtown Toronto, at Spadina and King Street. The space is about 11,000 square feet, and it used to house a massive liquor store, right in the middle of Canada's biggest city. For years, I worked down the street, and let me tell you, that liquor store was bumpin'. That's producer Chris Berube. I remember lines out the door before holidays, and setting aside a whole lunch hour just to get a bottle of wine. But in 2019, the liquor store moved to a new, bigger location close by, and the shop at Spadina and King closed. Since then, all 11,000 square feet have been empty. For a long time, I wondered,
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马尔斯。多伦多市中心有一个主要路口，位于斯帕迪纳街与国王街交汇处，那里有一家店面。

2
00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:12.000
okay, what is going to move in there? I mean, this is a big time space right downtown, but in 2019, it sat empty. 2020, still empty. 2021, empty. 2022, okay, you get the point. But it's only empty for about nine months out of the year because every fall, just like clockwork,
好吧，到底会有什么店搬进来呢？我的意思是，这可是市中心黄金地段的一处大空间，但在2019年，它一直闲置着。

3
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:44.000
the space has been possessed by a spirit, by spirit Halloween. If you live in the US or Canada, you have probably seen the orange, yellow, and black Spirit Halloween signs. They go up sometime in August in 1500 empty storefronts, and by early November, all of them disappear. Spirit Halloween has become something of an institution. If you aren't familiar, I mean, it's pretty simple.
这个空间被一个“幽灵”占据了——那就是“万圣节幽灵”店（Spirit Halloween）。如果你住在美加两地，你大概见过那些橙、黄、黑三色相间的“万圣节幽灵”招牌。

4
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:41.000
It's a pop-up store full of Halloween stuff. They sell Halloween masks, Halloween costumes, Halloween costumes for dogs, Halloween decorations, creepy robots, and several different kinds of fake blood. And it's successful. Spirit reportedly sells about a billion dollars worth of merchandise every year. For a long time, I resisted going to Spirit because, I don't know, it just seemed like a lot of loud noises and crowds and children running around. And I gotta say, when I finally took the plunge last fall, it was pretty much that. It's like it's set up to look like a crypt. It's just full of those electric candles that kind of look like fire. After passing through rows of plastic gravestones and being yelled at by a terrifying animatronic clown, I discovered this one blue jumpsuit costume that severely creeped me out.
这是一家摆满万圣节物品的快闪店。他们出售万圣节面具、万圣节服装、狗狗穿的万圣节服装、万圣节装饰品、令人毛骨悚然的机器人，以及好几种不同的假血浆。

5
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:11.000
So I see an avatar, which honestly is very disturbing. I can't imagine dressing a child like this. I don't like it. Overall, I'd say the experience wasn't really for me. But I know that my opinion is in the minority here, because there are a ton of people who are obsessed with Spirit Halloween. Today is a very special day, my friends, because today is the day that my local Spirit Halloween store has opened and we are here right in front of it about to go in.
所以我看到一个虚拟形象，老实说，这非常让人不安。我无法想象把一个孩子打扮成这样。我可不喜欢。

6
00:03:11.000 --> 00:04:01.000
I'm going to give you a full tour of this year's theme, animatronics. There are countless YouTube videos from creators who treat the first day of the Spirit calendar like a high holy day, walking through the aisles of a freshly opened store and exploring the new products. I love this guy. My name is Jackie and I have a YouTube channel called Super Enthused. Jackie's videos of Spirit Halloween walkthroughs have garnered hundreds of thousands of views. Jackie says the reason Spirit has developed a following is the way they've tried to make the store itself feel like a haunted house. One thing I found, like the first thing that hit me was that Spirit kind of let you play with things. They let you stomp on the floor and activate the
我将带大家完整参观一下今年的主题：电子动画。YouTube上无数的创作者将“万圣节幽灵”日历的第一天视为圣日，他们走过新开业店铺的过道，探索新产品。

7
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:21.000
animatronics and almost all the others, especially as a younger person, a kid, a teenager. No, no, no, don't touch! Don't touch that! Don't open that! You can't touch anything! You can't play with anything! You're watching you like a hawk if you're going to steal the whole store! But Spirit's like, come in, touch everything, play with all the things!
不，不，不，别碰！别碰那个！别打开那个！你什么都不能碰！你什么都不能玩！如果店里东西被偷了，他们盯着你就像盯着鹰一样！但“万圣节幽灵”却说：进来吧，随便摸，所有东西都可以玩！

8
00:04:21.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Spirit's haunted house energy is a big part of the draw, but there's also an element of scarcity. Because the real hallmark of Spirit Halloween is that it's only open roughly three months out of the year. Spirit Halloween has zero permanent year-round locations, and inside these pop-up stores, there's an overwhelming feeling that everything won't be around for long. Here is what I do not get about Spirit Halloween. The last few years have been considered this kind of retail apocalypse, with lots of brick and mortar
“万圣节幽灵”那种鬼屋般的氛围是吸引人的很大一部分原因，但其中也包含着一种稀缺元素。

9
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:42.000
chains closing their physical locations. A lot of those retailers have just left empty storefronts across the country. So what does it say about the world of retail, that a seasonal Halloween store is taking all these abandoned spaces for three months a year and making a killing in the process. To understand how Spirit became ubiquitous, I called it the guy who started it. Joe Marver opened Spirit in 1983. And I'll admit, when I met the father of Spirit Halloween, I kind of assumed he'd have a backstory about how he became a Halloween guy.
为了解“万圣节幽灵”是如何变得无处不在的，我打电话给它的创始人。乔·马维尔（Joe Marver）于1983年创立了“万圣节幽灵”。

10
00:05:42.000 --> 00:06:08.000
Like he stumbled into a crypt full of bats or he stumbled upon a pumpkin that was full of bats. But it turns out his childhood was disappointingly bat-free. Did trick or treating out of my house. I mean, we're going back to 1950 is when I was seven years old. My first costume was Zorro.
就像他偶然闯进了一个满是蝙蝠的地下墓穴，或者偶然发现了一个装满蝙蝠的南瓜。但事实证明，他的童年令人失望地没有任何蝙蝠。

11
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Okay, but Joe Marver did tell me as a teen, he attended summer camp at this place with a really spooky name. I went to a camp in Devil's Lake, Wisconsin. In his 20s, Joe Marver moved west to California in search of his fortune. He worked as a clown in Las Vegas, then at a shoe store, and by the early 80s he owned an outlet store in the Bay Area. A haunted outlet store.
好吧，不过乔·马维尔确实告诉我，他十几岁时曾在一个名字非常诡异的地方参加过夏令营。我曾在威斯康星州魔鬼湖的一个营地待过。

12
00:06:38.000 --> 00:07:12.000
It was not haunted. It was just a regular store called Spirit Discount Women's Apparel. But right across the parking lot from Joe's store was a dancewear shop. They sold leotards and tutus, but also a selection of Halloween costumes. And watching their customers gave Joe Marver an idea. They were lined up around the block for the month of October, and it was insane, because our October stunk in the women's apparel business across the street in a strip center. In 1983, the dancewear shop closed,
这里并不是闹鬼。这只是一家普通的店，名叫“幽灵折扣女装”。但在乔的店对面马路停车场，有一家舞蹈服装店。

13
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:40.000
and Joe Marver decided he could not let those customers go someplace else. So he put the women's clothing in the basement and bought a crop of Halloween costumes. Hmm, I'm gonna go to a trade show. I'm gonna get wigs, makeup, a lot of products that could be for Halloween. That October, Joe completely changed his business for one month only.
乔·马维尔决定不能让那些顾客去别的地方。于是他把女装搬到地下室，并采购了一批万圣节服装。

14
00:07:40.000 --> 00:08:18.000
And Spirit Discount Women's Apparel became Spirit Halloween. Our staff totally was dressing up in costume, something that might have been a costume that we wanted to sell. And it rocked. It was amazing. The new business model worked right away. In his second year, Joe says he made $100,000. Soon, he was running six stores, and then even more. And it's from these early stores that we get the not-built-to-last quality that's
“万圣节幽灵”折扣女装店变成了“万圣节幽灵”店。我们的员工完全盛装打扮成我们想卖的服装造型。而且效果棒极了。太惊艳了。

15
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:45.000
come to define Spirit Halloween. Now, this predates the rise of pop-up retailers. Back then, there wasn't this modern concept of a cool artisanal clothing shop that's open for two weeks, or a food truck in the parking lot of your favorite bar. Spirit was ahead of all that. And because they were just building for the short term, they used materials that could be put up and taken down really quickly. I remember that the tables for displays
现在，这比快闪零售商的兴起还要早。当时的市场上还没有这种现代概念：开业两周的酷炫手工艺服装店，或是你最喜欢的酒吧停车场里的餐车。

16
00:08:45.000 --> 00:09:04.000
were actually the cardboard boxes that the merchandise came in. Cause he ended up with all these boxes, lots of boxes, you know, maybe a thousand cartons at the time. And you'd pile these boxes up in the middle of the sales floor, drape a black cloth over them. And that was your display table, you know? So ultimate in reduce, reuse, recycle, right?
展示用的桌子其实就是商品运来时装货的纸箱子。因为他最后收集了所有这些箱子，很多的箱子，你知道的，当时可能有一千个纸箱。

17
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:34.000
That's Mike Olson, who started working with Spirit in the 80s. Mike says while the setup was a little bare bones, Joe Marver had a knack for making his stores feel macabre. You know, the aisles were nice and wide and you were just inundated with all the sights and sounds of Halloween. Joe started experimenting with how much he could scare customers without scaring them away. For example, many spirit stores included baby carriages with creepy dolls in the middle
那是迈克·奥尔森（Mike Olson），他从80年代开始与“万圣节幽灵”合作。迈克说，虽然店铺布置有点简陋，但乔·马维尔很擅长让他的店铺营造出一种阴森恐怖的氛围。

18
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:56.000
of the action. You had the animatronics, they step on the mat and they scream at you. Customers loved the mix of kitschy and creepy. But early on, Joe hit a major roadblock in expanding the business. Landlords just didn't understand the idea of a temporary store. Mike Olson says they associated the idea with something low rent and unreliable,
你有电子动画道具，他们一踩到垫子上就会尖叫。顾客们非常喜欢这种俗气与诡异结合的风格。

19
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:17.000
like say a firework stand that would pop up on the side of the road around the 4th of July. Very few landlords wanted to rent their space to a business that was going to put down stakes for eight weeks and then leave that space abandoned. He's calling around to landlords and other brokers and they look at you like you had three heads when you say, look, I've got this temporary Halloween thing.
很少有房东愿意把他们的空间租给一家只停留八周然后就把空间抛弃的商业企业。他四处打电话给房东和其他经纪人，当你说“听着，我有个临时的万圣节项目”时，他们看着你的眼神就像你长了三个脑袋一样。

20
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:45.000
To find locations, Mike Olsen would drive around town looking for shopping centers with empty rundown storefronts until he found one that might take his call. That was it. When you put it like that, it makes it sound like, well, that was super inefficient, but it was really the only way. Mike Olson says the sales pitch in the early days was pretty difficult. So the Sphere team got creative. Mike remembers trying to win over one corporate landlord with this suite of available spaces.
迈克·奥尔森说，早期的销售说辞相当困难。所以“万圣节幽灵”团队开始发挥创意。

21
00:10:45.000 --> 00:11:25.000
I just know that I wanted to get in and break that nut with him. And eventually called my buddy Anne Sullivan down at the Hayward office. I said, Hey, any chance that we've got a severed arm down in inventory somewhere? And she said, you know, I think we do. So she sends that thing to me and I staple it to a piece of cardboard, put my business card on it and then write on there the gentleman's name, hey, lend me a hand. I need your location here. And I put it in an envelope, sent it off to the guy. And he called me back and I got the deal. Ultimately, Spirit was able to solve their problem largely by paying above market rate
最终，“万圣节幽灵”很大程度上通过为短期租约支付高于市场价的租金，解决了他们的难题。

22
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:57.000
for their short-term leases. But another thing that helped Spirit was timing. Big box stores spread across America in the 1960s and 70s with the rise of Walmart, Target, and Kmart. By the 1980s, big box retail stores were becoming ubiquitous in the American suburbs. Those retailers put a lot of mom-and and pop and smaller chains out of business. But for Spirit Halloween, the ubiquity of these big boxes was an opportunity. Because if one of these stores closed, which happened from time to time, landlords were
到了20世纪80年代，大卖场零售店在美国家庭郊区变得无处不在。那些零售商让许多夫妻老婆店和小连锁店倒闭了。

23
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:29.000
stuck. These spaces were purpose-built for something like a Kmart, and they were difficult to repurpose. You couldn't just put in a hospital or an apartment complex into an old big box unless you completely redeveloped the property and got zoning laws changed. Mike Olson says Spirit became a favorite for corporate landlords because of their promise. We'll fill your empty boxes for a couple of months while you look for a new tenant and maybe we'll fix them up a little. We've cleaned more floors, replaced more light bulbs, and fixed more HVAC units across
迈克·奥尔森说，“万圣节幽灵”之所以成为大型房东的最爱，是因为他们做出了承诺。

24
00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:54.000
the country than I would think any other retailer at this point. Because when we go into a space, we need that space to look good. And oftentimes, a retailer, when they leave a space, they're not leaving it in a condition that's ready to occupy. This was Spirit's real estate strategy. Sometimes they'd take an old bank or one time a defunct church, but mostly they were laser focused on abandoned big boxes. Anything over 10,000 square feet,
这就是“万圣节幽灵”的房地产策略。有时他们会租用老旧的银行，或者有一次是一座废弃的教堂，但他们绝大部分精力都高度集中在废弃的大型仓储卖场上。任何超过11,000平方英尺的空间，

25
00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:38.000
preferably across the parking lot from another popular store. If it had good traffic and it was a corner that had across the street a Lowe's or a Home Depot, then that would be enough of a draw. Now we're at a Walmart just off the highway with highway visibility with an exit. Hello, money. By the 90s, Spirit had a few dozen stores. They even managed to secure the perfect celebrity spokesperson. I even had Elvira, Mistress of the Dark, who we put on our billboards with her picture,
如果人流量好，且街角对面有劳氏（Lowe's）或家得宝（Home Depot），那就足够吸引人了。现在我们在高速公路旁的一家沃尔玛，交通便利且靠近出口。

26
00:13:38.000 --> 00:14:22.000
and I got rights to the phone number 1-800-HALLOWEEN. Spirit went national, and pretty soon a big mall brand took notice. Fast forward to 1998, and Spencer Gifts approaches me and says, we would like to talk to you about possibly doing a deal. After 16 years in business, Joe sold Spirit Halloween to Spencer's, a store most Americans will know for their fine array of lava lamps and novelty t-shirts. Joe stayed on as a consultant for a while,
在经营了16年后，乔将“万圣节幽灵”卖给了斯彭斯（Spencer's），这家店大多数美国人对它的印象是出售各种精致的熔岩灯和新奇T恤。

27
00:14:22.000 --> 00:15:14.000
but today, Joe Marver is retired from the company, and he runs a hotel in Washington state. He still dresses up every year for Halloween, and he plays a character for his guests. And I don't do too bad on Elvis voice. People who work for Spirit Halloween talk about a before and an after for the company. Under corporate ownership, Spirit has gone from 65 stores to 1,500, and they moved away from some of the generic costumes they used to carry. Old Spirit would feature largely off-brand costumes. You might've seen jokes about these online,
但今天，乔·马维尔已经从公司退休，他在华盛顿州经营着一家酒店。他每年万圣节仍然会盛装打扮，为他的客人扮演一个角色。我的猫王唱腔学得还算不错。

28
00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:40.000
like the sidekick bros who look suspiciously like Super Mario and Luigi, but today Spirit has the license to real characters from Disney and Marvel and most of the popular horror franchises. Spirit Halloween have corporate owners now and a website where you can buy stuff all year round. But their physical stores are still a temporary operation. Their logo is still plastered on a tarp in front of each storefront.
如今，“万圣节幽灵”有了企业所有者，还有一个全年都可以网购的网站。但他们的实体店仍然是临时运营。他们的 logo 仍然贴在每个店面门前的防水布上。

29
00:15:40.000 --> 00:16:09.000
Their display is still made out of pegboard and they are still only open a few months a year. The biggest thing that stayed the same about Spirit Halloween is their real estate plan. They keep searching for empty big box stores every year. Here's Mike Olson. You know, I'm cruising the newspaper and I see someone file for bankruptcy. Admittedly, I'm immediately jumping on the, you know, say, okay, where are the stores, how big are they, and can I get, can I take advantage of this? Hey, I'm immediately jumping on the, you know, say, okay, where are the stores? How big are they? And can I get to kind of take advantage of this? Hey, I'm a broker. I can admit that. I'll go with that.
迈克·奥尔森说：你知道的，我在浏览报纸时看到有人申请破产。我得承认，我马上就会跳出来，说，好吧，店在哪里，它们有多大，我能不能利用这个机会？

30
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:47.000
You know, it's it's finding opportunities for my client. Spirit has benefited whenever there has been a crisis in the retail sector, first with the wave of corporate closures in the 90s. Good guys in CompUSA and Circuit City and Best Buy was out there, Barnes & Noble, Borders Books, all of the big box, you know, the category killers. Then, there was a much bigger crisis in the American economy. Get to 2008 financial meltdown, 2009, I think the Phoenix metro area at one time had 256 empty retail boxes, big boxes. Take your pick.
接着，美国经济迎来了更大的危机。到了2008年的金融崩盘，2009年，我记得菲尼克斯都会区一度有256个空置的零售大卖场。任君挑选。

31
00:16:47.000 --> 00:17:07.000
Many brands didn't survive the financial downturn or lost out to Amazon or other online retailers. And Spirit has taken many of their old storefronts. Yeah, we could take old Sears boxes, old Kmart boxes. You've probably seen those, you know, the 40,000 square foot Toys R Us box or the 30,000 square foot Bed Bath and Beyond. This kind of guerrilla adaptive reuse
这种游击式的适应性再利用，让一些批评者称“万圣节幽灵”为美国零售业的死神。事实上，整件事在网络上变成了一个笑话。

32
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:28.000
has led some critics to call Spirit the Grim Reaper of American retail. In fact, the whole thing has become a joke online. Yeah, we're part of the meme universe. You probably see the memes out there that whenever the government shuts down, for example, someone hangs the Spirit Halloween banner, superimposes on the White House.
作为一家企业，“万圣节幽灵”发展得很好。但我想知道城市政策专家对此有何看法。雷切尔·奎德诺（Rachel Quednow）是“坚固城镇”（Strong Towns）组织的节目总监，该组织倡导反对郊区式的规划。她并不喜欢“万圣节幽灵”所代表的一切。

33
00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:55.000
As a business, Spirit is doing well for itself. But I wanted to know what urban policy folks think about it. Rachel Quednow is the program director at Strong Towns, an organization that advocates against suburban-style planning. And she doesn't like what spirit represents. If our cities are thinking about like, what is a long-term benefit for us financially, for our residents, then we shouldn't be thinking
